{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Give her of the fruit of her hands, and let her works praise her in the gates</b> (Proverbs 31:31)." "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, in the section sealed with this verse (Proverbs 31), began: אשת חיל מי ימצא — 'A woman of valor, who can find?' By way of the plain sense (peshat) he speaks of a wise woman, diligent in the affairs of her household, exerting herself and toiling at her craft by day and by night; as it says (ibid.): טעמה כי טוב סחרה לא יכבה בלילה נרה. She is renowned for the goodness of her conduct toward the poor, strengthening their hands and sustaining them from the labor of her own hands; as it says (ibid.): כפה פרשה לעני וידיה שלחה לאביון. And from this section we learn moral instruction and proper conduct: that a man should strive after a good wife, for she is the foundation of the house and its mainstay, and with her it is built and established. He wished to mention in the section her praiseworthy qualities together with a remembrance of all the letters of the aleph-bet inscribed there, in order to explain that the good woman who embraces good qualities embraces the entire Torah, for she is the preparation for a man to attain the success of Torah and commandments, just as the body is a preparation for the soul toward the soul's success, as our Sages of blessed memory said: 'If he is worthy, she is a help.' And concerning this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 18): מצא אשה מצא טוב ויפק רצון מה'. And since Scripture mentioned concerning the first man when he was alone (Genesis 2): לא טוב — 'not good' — therefore Solomon mentioned, concerning the matter of pairing, מצא טוב — 'found good.' And since a woman is not fit to be praised for grace or beauty except when she is a God-fearer, may He be blessed, which is what he said (Proverbs 31): שקר החן והבל היופי אשה יראת ה' היא תתהלל — therefore he concluded his words openly: תנו לה מפרי ידיה ויהללוה בשערים מעשיה — that is, it is fitting that they give her honor from the fruit of her hands, from what she has stored and profited, and that people praise her in the gates for the beauty of her deeds and the excellence of her actions — as is known in the case of Abigail, who through her diligence and good sense saved her husband and all the members of his household from the sword of David and his men, as David said to her (1 Samuel 25): וברוך טעמך וברוכה את אשר כליתיני היום הזה מבא בדמים." "<b>And by way</b> of the Midrash: תנו לה מפרי ידיה — 'Give her of the fruit of her hands' — refers back to אשת חיל מי ימצא, and it is a parable about the Torah and wisdom, which not all attain; and he goes on enumerating the praise of her virtues: בטח בה לב בעלה — this is the wise man who engages in her, and therefore it says ושלל לא יחסר, as is known of wisdom, that all who engage in her matters constantly lack nothing through her. And the עני ואביון — 'poor and needy' — that he mentioned are the disciples, who are deficient in wisdom until they exert themselves in her and attain her. And therefore it says: תנו לה מפרי ידיה — for it is fitting that all creatures give her honor and a crown from the sweet and pleasant fruit that emerges from her, and these are the chosen deeds through which a person merits eternal life; as it is written concerning the wise man (Proverbs 11:30): פרי צדיק עץ חיים — for the fruit of the righteous, which is the Torah, is עץ חיים — a tree of life — for it is what brings him to life, ואכל וחי לעולם." "<b>And by way</b> of the Kabbalah: תנו לה מפרי ידיה — he mentioned this concerning the attribute with which he began upon her: אשת חיל מי ימצא, for Solomon, peace be upon him, wished to seal his book with the attribute that is the seal of the structure. And since from there Israel heard the Torah, as it is written (Deuteronomy 4): מתוך האש, and the prophet said (Habakkuk 3): נתן תהום קולו — therefore he arranged her praises and mentioned her virtues in the 22 letters of the Torah. And the intent of the verse תנו לה מפרי ידיה is: give her blessing, and he mentioned מפרי ידיה in the manner of what they said: 'Give him of what is his — you and what is yours are his.' ויהללוה בשערים מעשיה — this is praise. And thus David, peace be upon him, mentioned in one verse blessing and praise, as he said (Psalms 104): ברכי נפשי את ה' הללויה. And even though all who are blessed are blessed from the blessings of the Holy One, blessed be He, we find that the Holy One, blessed be He, desires the blessing of human beings and He commanded us in this, as He said (Deuteronomy 8): וברכת את ה' אלהיך, and He, may He be exalted, is the one who blesses (Isaiah 65): והמתברך בארץ יתברך באלהי אמן. And you should be roused by what our Sages of blessed memory expounded: כל המברך מתברך — 'all who bless are blessed' — which is a general principle that embraces everything. And it is known of blessing that it is the maintenance of the world, and therefore the Torah began with ב, in the creation of the world, because blessing is the maintenance of the world. And the Holy One, blessed be He, blessed man, as it says (Genesis 1): ויברך אותם אלהים, and He blessed Noah and his sons, as it says (ibid. 9): ויברך אלהים את נח ואת בניו, until Abraham stood, who was the head of faith, and He delivered the blessings into his hand and said to him (ibid. 12): והיה ברכה — and He did not say והיה ברוך or מבורך, but והיה ברכה; He said to him: You are the source of blessing, to bless whomever you wish. And from him onward the blessings were entrusted to the hands of the righteous — thus it is written of Isaac, who said to Jacob (ibid. 27): ואברככה לפני ה' לפני מותי, and likewise we find of Jacob, that he blessed his sons close to his death, as it is written (ibid. 49): וזאת אשר דבר להם אביהם ויברך אותם, and likewise we find Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, that he blessed the tribes close to his death, and he began his blessing with the word זאת, from the place where Jacob left off, who said וזאת אשר דבר להם אביהם. And this is what is written:" "<b>And this is the blessing with which Moses the man of Elohim blessed the children of Israel before his death.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): Moses arranged this blessing and blessed in it all the tribes of Israel on the day of his death, for so is the way of the righteous — to arrange their blessing on the day of their death." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> 'And this is the blessing' is an addition to the blessings with which Balaam blessed Israel. For it was fitting to bless them seven blessings, corresponding to the seven altars, but he blessed them only three, as it says (Numbers 24): ״והנה ברכת ברך זה שלש פעמים״. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: Wicked one, your eye is grudging in blessing them; so I too will not permit you through My hand to complete your blessings for Israel. Let Moses come, whose eyes are fine for blessing them — and this is what is written (Mishlei 22): ״טוב עין הוא יבורך״ — do not read יבורך but rather יברך; this is Moses our teacher, whose eyes were fine to bless Israel. And he blessed them four blessings: the first (Shemot 39): ״וירא משה את כל המלאכה והנה עשו אותה כאשר צוה ה' את משה ויברך אתם משה״; the second (Vayikra 9): ״ויבא משה ואהרן אל אהל מועד ויצאו ויברכו את העם״; the third (Devarim 1): ״ה' אלהי אבותיכם יסף עליכם ככם אלף פעמים ויברך אתכם״; the fourth — 'וזאת הברכה.'" "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah.</b> 'And this is the blessing' — it is the terumah and it is the Torah and it is the covenant, of which it is written (Isaiah 59): ״זאת בריתי״. And therefore Moses began from it, from the place where Jacob left off, for there was the cessation of prophecy to Jacob and from there was the beginning of prophecy to Moses. And I have already mentioned this in connection with the verse (Genesis 49): ״וזאת אשר דבר להם אביהם״." "<b>A man of Elohim.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the greatness of prophecy is in [the name] Elohim, for even at the beginning of his prophecy at the bush, Elohim was revealed to him." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> 'A man of Elohim' — a man of judgment, as it says: ״צדקת ה' עשה ומשפטיו עם ישראל״, for he would say, 'Let the judgment be established upon the mountain.' Rabbi Yehudah bar Simon said: Why was he called 'a man of Elohim'? Just as this man — if he wishes to annul his wife's vows, he annuls, and if he wishes, he upholds, as it says (Numbers 30): ״אישה יקימנו ואישה יפרנו״ — so Moses, as it were, said to the Holy One, blessed be He (ibid. 10): ״קומה ה' שובה ה'״. End." "<b>You should be roused</b> to ask what was the intent of this Midrash. For since the prophecy of Moses emanated from the attribute of mercy, which is compared to the agent, Scripture compared Moses and the attribute of judgment to agent and recipient, because one who cleaves to a thing is called by its name." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ״איש האלהים״ — the way of Scripture is to use the term איש (man) together with האלהים, for in all the Torah one does not find ״איש ה׳״, but rather the term עבד (servant) is used together with YHWH, which is the special Name; and this is ״משה עבד ה׳״ — and this is clear." "<b>Before his death.</b> That is, close to his death. <b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״לפני מותו״ — the angel of death came to take his soul; Moses delayed him, rose and blessed them, and this is ״לפני מותו״ — before the angel who would cause him to die."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :register {:flags {1 ["God"]}}, :notes {8 "The prooftext 'צדקת ה׳ עשה ומשפטיו עם ישראל' is unattributed by R. Bahya; it appears to combine Deuteronomy 33:21 wording. The phrase 'יקוב הדין את ההר' is a well-known rabbinic dictum; rendered as printed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:54:21.858102405Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}